// THE LATE REPUBLIC (264–27 BC) — full English content. Same structure and same image slots as the Portuguese version (dados.js).
// Chronology: conventional dates (BC); exact days of battles and events follow ancient tradition (Polybius, Livy, Appian, Plutarch) and some are debated.

const visao = [
  { caixa: 'In brief', texto: [
    'In 264 BC, Rome was mistress of the whole Italian peninsula, but still only a regional power. In 27 BC, when Octavian received the name **Augustus**, Rome ruled the whole Mediterranean, from Spain to Egypt and from Gaul to Syria. Between those two dates lie 237 years and one of the fastest and most violent transformations in ancient history: the **Late Republic**.',
    'It is the age of the **Punic Wars** against Carthage (264–146 BC), of the destruction of Carthage and Corinth (146 BC), of the conquest of Greece, Spain and the East, of the **Gracchi**, **Marius** and **Sulla**, **Spartacus**, **Pompey**, **Crassus** and **Caesar**, **Cicero**, the civil wars and, finally, the battle of **Actium** (31 BC). It was also the time when the institutions of a city-state could no longer govern an empire, and the Republic died, not in a single blow, but by attrition.'
  ] },
  { img: 'rrt-mapa-expansao', leg: 'Map of the Roman Republic in 44 BC, at the end of Republican expansion.' },
  { h: 'Where and when' },
  'This page begins where the page on the **Monarchy and Early Republic** ends: the defeat of Pyrrhus (275 BC) and Roman rule over Italy. It ends in 27 BC, when the Senate gave Octavian the name Augustus: the year conventionally taken as the start of the Principate (see the page **The High Empire**). It is not a sharp boundary: contemporaries did not know the Republic had ended, and Augustus himself presented his power as the “restoration of the Republic”.',
  { tabela: { cab: ['Phase', 'Dates', 'Main landmarks'], linhas: [
    ['Punic Wars and conquest of the western Mediterranean', '264 – 146 BC', 'First Punic War (264–241), Hannibal and the Second (218–201), Zama (202), destruction of Carthage (146)'],
    ['Conquest of the East and of Spain', '215 – 133 BC', 'Macedonian wars, Pydna (168), Corinth (146), Viriathus (147–139), Numantia (133)'],
    ['The crisis: the Gracchi and Marius', '133 – 100 BC', 'Tiberius and Gaius Gracchus, Jugurthine War, Cimbri and Teutones, Marius’ reforms'],
    ['Sulla’s civil wars', '91 – 78 BC', 'Social War, Mithridates, march on Rome, proscriptions, Sulla’s dictatorship'],
    ['The age of Pompey and Cicero', '78 – 60 BC', 'Sertorius, Spartacus, pirates, conquest of the East, Catiline’s conspiracy'],
    ['Caesar and the civil wars', '60 – 44 BC', 'Triumvirate, Gaul, Rubicon, Pharsalus, dictatorship, Ides of March'],
    ['The end of the Republic', '44 – 27 BC', 'Second Triumvirate, Philippi, Antony and Cleopatra, Actium, Augustus']
  ] } },
  { h: 'What the Republic was like' },
  'The Roman Republic had no written constitution. It worked through **magistrates elected for one year** (two **consuls** at the head, then praetors, aediles and quaestors), the **tribunes of the plebs** (who could veto measures), the assemblies of the people and, above all, the **Senate**, a lifelong council of former magistrates that dominated foreign policy, finance and religion. It was a system designed to stop any one man from having too much power. It worked as long as Rome was a city-state that fought wars in spring and summer. It stopped working when generals spent years away, commanding armies loyal more to them than to the state.',
  { img: 'rrt-forum-romano', leg: 'Roman Forum, view of the present ruins.' },
  { h: 'Why these two centuries matter' },
  { lista: [
    '**How an empire is made:** provinces, governors, taxes, private tax collectors (the publicani), slaves in bulk, roads and colonies: a model that lasted for centuries and that Europe studied and imitated.',
    '**The debate about power:** the idea of mixed government, of separation of powers, of limited terms, and the fear of the “tyrant”, passed from republican Rome to Machiavelli, to the Founding Fathers of the United States and to the French Revolution.',
    '**Words and institutions:** “senate”, “consul”, “veto”, “dictator”, “candidate”, “campaign”, “republic” (res publica, “public thing”).',
    '**Literature:** Cicero, Caesar, Lucretius, Catullus, Sallust, Plautus and Terence wrote works in Latin that are still read today.',
    '**Portugal:** it was in this period that the Romans reached the territory of present-day Portugal and that Viriathus resisted them.'
  ] },
  { img: 'rrt-triunfo-republicano', leg: 'AI-generated illustration. Roman triumph in the Late Republic, c. 60 BC; interpretive scene.' },
  { h: 'A history with many sources, each with an author' },
  'Almost everything we know about this period comes from ancient authors with positions of their own. **Polybius** (a Greek, hostage in Rome, witness of Carthage and Corinth) is the most reliable historian of the expansion. **Livy** (writing already in Augustus’ time) tells the wars with talent and patriotism. **Sallust** was a Caesarian. **Caesar** wrote his own Commentaries, which are also propaganda. **Cicero** left hundreds of letters and speeches that show politics from the inside. **Appian** and **Plutarch** wrote centuries later, but from sources we have lost. Whenever a number or a date comes from an ancient source and is doubtful, this page says so.',
  { h: 'Today' },
  'The Late Republic is perhaps the most studied period of Antiquity for its politics: polarisation, the resort to violence, the use of money to buy votes, the erosion of norms, the popular general who defies the Senate. But parallels should not be forced. Rome was a slave society in which a minority of free men voted; its “democracy” was limited and heavily skewed towards the rich. What makes the story so instructive is not the parallel with the present, but the slowness with which institutions were hollowed out.'
];

const linha = [
  'This timeline follows 237 years of history. Dates are the conventional ones, in years BC; for exact days the Roman calendar of the time is used, which before Caesar’s reform (46 BC) did not match the seasons well. Figures for troops and casualties come from ancient sources and are generally exaggerated or uncertain.',
  { linha: [
    { d: '264 BC', t: 'Start of the First Punic War', x: 'Campanian mercenaries (the Mamertines) occupy Messina in Sicily and ask Rome for help against Syracuse and Carthage. Rome sends troops outside Italy for the first time and goes to war with Carthage, the great naval power. According to tradition, it is also the year of the first gladiatorial combats in Rome, at a funeral.' },
    { d: '260 BC', t: 'Rome builds a fleet and wins at Mylae', x: 'According to Polybius, the Romans copied a captured Carthaginian ship, built about 100 quinqueremes and invented the corvus, a bridge with a hook that turned naval combat into infantry combat. Gaius Duilius’ victory at Mylae was the first great Roman naval victory.' },
    { d: '241 BC', t: 'End of the First Punic War', x: 'After twenty-three years, the victory at the Aegates Islands forces Carthage to ask for peace: it abandons Sicily and pays a heavy indemnity. Sicily becomes the first Roman **province** (Sardinia and Corsica follow around 238–227 BC).' },
    { d: '218 BC', t: 'Hannibal crosses the Alps', x: 'After taking Saguntum in Spain (219), Hannibal leaves Cartagena and leads an army, with elephants, across the Pyrenees, the Rhône and the Alps to invade Italy. He beats the Romans at the Ticinus and the Trebia. The exact size of his army varies by source.' },
    { d: '216 BC', t: 'Cannae', x: 'At Cannae, in Apulia, the Carthaginian army surrounds and destroys the Roman legions: ancient sources give between c. 48,000 and 70,000 Roman dead, and several thousand prisoners (Polybius gives c. 10,000). It is one of the greatest defeats in Roman history. But most of the allies in central Italy remain loyal, and Rome refuses to negotiate.' },
    { d: '212 – 211 BC', t: 'Syracuse and Capua', x: 'The Romans take Syracuse after a long siege; the scholar **Archimedes** dies in the sack (the story of the soldier who kills him is tradition). In 211, Capua, the great city that had gone over to Hannibal, surrenders and is severely punished.' },
    { d: '209 – 206 BC', t: 'Scipio conquers Carthaginian Spain', x: 'Publius Cornelius Scipio, later called “Africanus”, takes Cartagena (Carthago Nova) by surprise in 209 and wins at Ilipa (206). The Carthaginians are expelled from Spain: a Roman presence of more than six centuries in the Peninsula begins. In 206 Italica is founded, near Seville.' },
    { d: '202 BC', t: 'Zama', x: 'Scipio lands in Africa and defeats Hannibal at Zama. The peace of 201 reduces Carthage to a city without empire or fleet. Hannibal governs the city for some years and will eventually die in exile, in Bithynia, around 183 BC.' },
    { d: '197 – 168 BC', t: 'The wars in the East', x: 'Rome defeats Philip V of Macedon at Cynoscephalae (197); in 196, Flamininus proclaims the “freedom of the Greeks”. Antiochus III of Syria is beaten at Magnesia (190). At Pydna (168), Aemilius Paullus defeats Perseus, the last king of Macedon; Macedonia is divided into four republics.' },
    { d: '155 – 139 BC', t: 'Lusitanian War and Viriathus', x: 'The Lusitanians, between the Tagus and the Douro, resist Rome. Around 150, the governor Servius Galba treacherously kills thousands of Lusitanians. **Viriathus**, who escaped, leads the resistance from c. 147 to 139 BC, until he is murdered by three companions bribed by the consul Servilius Caepio.' },
    { d: '149 – 146 BC', t: 'Third Punic War; Carthage and Corinth destroyed', x: 'After years of pressure (tradition attributes to Cato the Elder the refrain “Carthage must be destroyed”), Rome attacks Carthage. Scipio Aemilianus takes it in 146. The city is razed and its lands become the province of **Africa**. In the same year, Mummius destroys Corinth and Greece falls under Roman control. (The salt supposedly sown on Carthage’s lands is a modern invention.)' },
    { d: '133 BC', t: 'Numantia and Pergamum; Tiberius Gracchus', x: 'Scipio Aemilianus takes Numantia after several years of war in Celtiberia. King Attalus III bequeaths the kingdom of Pergamum to Rome (the province of Asia is born around 129). In Rome, the tribune Tiberius Gracchus proposes a land reform and is murdered by senators, in one of the first violent political deaths of the Republic.' },
    { d: '123 – 121 BC', t: 'Gaius Gracchus', x: 'Tiberius’ brother, elected tribune twice, proposes laws on grain, courts, colonies and citizenship. In 121, the Senate passes the emergency decree (senatus consultum ultimum) and Gaius dies with thousands of supporters. Around 121–118 Transalpine Gaul (the future Narbonensis) is born.' },
    { d: '112 – 105 BC', t: 'War against Jugurtha', x: 'The Numidian king Jugurtha buys senators and defeats generals. Gaius Marius, a “new man”, is elected consul in 107 and ends the war with the help of Sulla, his quaestor. The war exposed the corruption of the elite and the strength of a popular general.' },
    { d: '105 – 101 BC', t: 'Cimbri and Teutones; Marius’ reforms', x: 'Germanic tribes defeat Rome at Arausio (105), one of the worst defeats since Cannae. Marius, consul repeatedly (104–100), reorganises the army and beats them at Aquae Sextiae (102) and Vercellae (101). From then on legionaries are also recruited among the poor and depend on the general for their retirement land.' },
    { d: '91 – 88 BC', t: 'Social War', x: 'The Italian allies, who fought for Rome but were not citizens, rebel. Rome yields: the Julian law (90) and others extend citizenship to Italians who had not rebelled or who surrendered, and, within a few years, to most of Italy.' },
    { d: '88 – 82 BC', t: 'Mithridates, Sulla and civil war', x: 'Mithridates VI of Pontus invades Asia and in 88 orders the massacre of Romans and Italians in the province (sources speak of 80,000 dead, a figure that is disputed). Sulla, consul, marches on Rome with his army (88), for the first time in the city’s history. A civil war with Marius and Cinna follows; Sulla wins at the Colline Gate (82).' },
    { d: '82 – 79 BC', t: 'Sulla’s dictatorship', x: 'Sulla has himself appointed dictator “to write laws and reorganise the Republic”, proscribes his enemies (lists of names whose property was confiscated and whose killers were rewarded), strengthens the Senate and limits the tribunes. In 79 he abdicates, a rare event in history, and dies in 78.' },
    { d: '73 – 71 BC', t: 'The revolt of Spartacus', x: 'Slaves escaped from a gladiator school at Capua form an army of tens of thousands (Appian speaks of c. 120,000, a doubtful figure). They defeat several legions. Crassus beats them in 71; six thousand prisoners are crucified along the Appian Way, according to Appian.' },
    { d: '67 – 63 BC', t: 'Pompey in the East; Cicero consul', x: 'Pompey receives extraordinary powers to eliminate the pirates of the Mediterranean (67) and then to defeat Mithridates (66). He reorganises the East: Syria becomes a province (64) and he takes Jerusalem (63). In Rome, Cicero, consul in 63, denounces Catiline’s conspiracy.' },
    { d: '60 – 53 BC', t: 'The First Triumvirate', x: 'Caesar, Pompey and Crassus make a private political agreement (it never had a legal basis, and the word “triumvirate” is modern). Caesar is consul in 59 and then governor of Gaul. Julia, Caesar’s daughter and Pompey’s wife, dies in 54; Crassus dies at Carrhae (53), defeated by the Parthians.' },
    { d: '58 – 50 BC', t: 'The conquest of Gaul', x: 'Caesar conquers Gaul and writes the Commentaries on the Gallic War. He crosses the Rhine and the English Channel (55 and 54). In 52 he besieges Vercingetorix at Alesia, with two lines of fortifications, in one of the great siege operations of Antiquity.' },
    { d: '49 BC', t: 'The Rubicon', x: 'When the Senate orders him to disband his army, Caesar crosses the Rubicon, the river marking the limit of his province, with one legion (10 or 11 January). The phrase “the die is cast” is tradition, and its exact form varies. Pompey and most of the Senate leave Italy.' },
    { d: '48 – 45 BC', t: 'Pharsalus, Thapsus, Munda', x: 'Caesar beats Pompey at Pharsalus, in Greece, in 48; Pompey is murdered on arriving in Egypt. Caesar stays in Alexandria with Cleopatra and defeats the Pompeians at Thapsus (46) and Munda, in Spain (45). In 46 he reforms the calendar.' },
    { d: '44 BC', t: 'The Ides of March', x: 'Appointed dictator for life in February, Caesar is assassinated on 15 March by more than sixty conspirators, among them Brutus and Cassius, at a meeting of the Senate in the Curia of Pompey. They had no plan for the following day.' },
    { d: '43 – 42 BC', t: 'The Second Triumvirate and Philippi', x: 'Octavian, Mark Antony and Lepidus receive legal powers for five years and proscribe thousands of enemies, among them Cicero, killed in December 43. In 42, at Philippi, in Macedonia, they defeat Brutus and Cassius, who commit suicide.' },
    { d: '31 – 30 BC', t: 'Actium and Alexandria', x: 'On 2 September 31, Octavian’s fleet, commanded by Agrippa, defeats that of Antony and Cleopatra at Actium, in Greece. In the summer of 30, Octavian enters Alexandria; Antony and Cleopatra commit suicide. Egypt becomes the personal possession of the future emperor.' },
    { d: '27 BC', t: 'Octavian becomes Augustus', x: 'On 13 January, Octavian declares that he is returning power to the Senate and the people; the Senate gives him important provinces with most of the legions and, on 16 January, the name **Augustus**. The Principate begins: the Republic, in form, continues; in reality, it is over.' }
  ] },
  { img: 'rrt-mapa-guerras-punicas', leg: 'Map of battles and Hannibal’s route during the Second Punic War.' },
  { img: 'rrt-galera-corvus', leg: 'AI-generated illustration. First Punic War naval battle, c. 260 BC: boarding with the corvus; conjectural interpretation.' },
  { img: 'rrt-anibal-busto', leg: 'Historical photograph of the Capua bust traditionally attributed to Hannibal, National Archaeological Museum of Naples. The identification is uncertain.' },
  { img: 'rrt-canas-batalha', leg: 'AI-generated illustration. Battle of Cannae, 216 BC; panoramic interpretation.' },
  { img: 'rrt-denario-idos-marco', leg: 'Silver denarius of Brutus with the inscription EID MAR, 42 BC, British Museum (1860,0328.124).' }
];

const mapa = [
  'The Late Republic can be measured in maps: each conquest is a province, each province a source of taxes, slaves and rivalry between generals. This section presents the cities, battlefields and routes that tell the story of 264 to 27 BC, and how all this connects to the territory of Portugal.',
  { tabela: { cab: ['City', 'Latin / ancient name', 'Role in the Late Republic', 'Location today'], linhas: [
    ['Rome', 'Roma', 'Capital, seat of the Senate and the assemblies; between 500,000 and 1 million inhabitants at the end of the period (much-debated estimate)', 'Rome, Italy'],
    ['Carthage', 'Karthago', 'Great maritime power, Rome’s rival; destroyed in 146 BC and refounded by Caesar and Augustus', 'Carthage, Tunisia'],
    ['Syracuse', 'Syracusae', 'Main Greek city of Sicily; taken in 212 BC', 'Syracuse, Sicily'],
    ['Capua', 'Capua', 'Campanian city, went over to Hannibal and was punished; gladiator school of Spartacus', 'Santa Maria Capua Vetere, Italy'],
    ['Cartagena', 'Carthago Nova', 'Carthaginian base in Spain, taken by Scipio in 209 BC', 'Cartagena, Spain'],
    ['Numantia', 'Numantia', 'Celtiberian city, taken in 133 BC after a long siege', 'Garray, Soria, Spain'],
    ['Corinth', 'Corinthus', 'Greek city destroyed in 146 BC; refounded by Caesar in 44', 'Corinth, Greece'],
    ['Pergamum', 'Pergamum', 'Kingdom bequeathed to Rome in 133 BC (province of Asia)', 'Bergama, Turkey'],
    ['Massilia', 'Massalia', 'Greek city, ally of Rome, which supported Pompey and was taken by Caesar in 49', 'Marseille, France'],
    ['Alesia', 'Alesia', 'Last stronghold of Vercingetorix, 52 BC', 'Alise-Sainte-Reine, France'],
    ['Alexandria', 'Alexandria', 'Capital of Ptolemaic Egypt, setting of the war between Caesar and Cleopatra', 'Alexandria, Egypt'],
    ['Olisipo', 'Olisipo', 'Lusitanian settlement on the banks of the Tagus; later became Felicitas Iulia (date debated)', 'Lisbon, Portugal']
  ] } },
  { img: 'rrt-cartago-ruinas', leg: 'Antonine Baths, Carthage, Tunisia.' },
  { h: 'Rome and Italy' },
  'At the centre of the map is **Rome**, a city of hills, a river, a Forum of narrow streets, the Capitol with its temple of Jupiter and, on the plain, the Campus Martius, where the assemblies and the army met. Its network of **roads** (the Appian Way, the Flaminian Way, the Aemilian Way) linked the capital to Italy and allowed legions to move. The peninsula was divided among Roman citizens, **Latin colonies** and **allies** (the socii), who supplied soldiers. It was this network of allies that gave Rome the reserve of men needed to beat Hannibal.',
  { img: 'rrt-via-appia', leg: 'Via Appia Antica with basalt paving.' },
  { h: 'Carthage and North Africa' },
  'Founded, according to tradition, by colonists from Tyre in the 9th century BC (the traditional date is 814 BC), **Carthage** dominated trade in the western Mediterranean, with a circular harbour for its war fleet. It was defeated in the First Punic War, lost Sicily and, after Zama, lost its whole empire. In 146 BC it was taken, sacked and razed; its inhabitants were sold as slaves. The region became the Roman province of **Africa**, one of Rome’s main sources of grain. The city was refounded as a Roman colony by Caesar (plans of 44 BC) and by Augustus, and would again become a great city.',
  { h: 'Sicily: the first laboratory of empire' },
  '**Sicily**, the first province (c. 241 BC), became Rome’s granary and the example of how a conquered territory was governed: with a governor, tax collectors who levied a tenth of the harvests and a local administration that took advantage of the Greek cities. In 70 BC, Cicero’s prosecution of **Verres**, a governor accused of plundering the province, showed in detail the abuses that were possible. Sicily was also where the first two great slave revolts broke out, in 135–132 and 104–100 BC.',
  { h: 'Spain and the territory of Portugal' },
  'Spain was the first great continental territory conquered outside Italy in which Rome stayed until the end of the Western Empire. After the expulsion of the Carthaginians (206 BC) two provinces were created, **Hispania Citerior** and **Ulterior** (197 BC). The interior and the northwest resisted for more than a century: the Celtiberian wars (181–133 BC), ending with the fall of **Numantia**, and the Lusitanian wars. The territory of present-day Portugal was divided among many peoples (Lusitanians, Gallaecians, Cynetes, Celtici); the Romans arrived south of the Tagus, and only in Augustus’ time was the conquest of the northwest completed.',
  { img: 'rrt-numancia-ruinas', leg: 'Ruins of Numantia, Garray, Soria.' },
  { h: 'Viriathus and Lusitania' },
  '**Viriathus** is the first great figure in the history of the Portuguese territory. According to the sources (Appian, Diodorus), he was a shepherd, then a hunter and bandit chief, who became the leader of the Lusitanians after Galba’s massacre (c. 150 BC). For about eight years he beat Roman armies with ambushes and mobile warfare, and even imposed a treaty on Rome (c. 141 BC), which Rome later broke. He was murdered in 139 BC by three companions, **Audax**, **Ditalcus** and **Minurus**, bribed by the consul Caepio; the Romans refused them the promised reward. In 138–136 BC, **Decimus Junius Brutus** campaigned as far as the Douro and Gallaecia; according to Strabo, he is said to have fortified Olisipo and the Tagus line. The location of the “Mount of Venus” and other places is much debated.',
  { img: 'rrt-viriato-viseu', leg: 'Monument to Viriathus in Viseu, by Mariano Benlliure, inaugurated in 1940.' },
  { h: 'Sertorius, Caesar and the end of the conquest' },
  'In the 70s BC, Spain was the stage for the war of **Sertorius**, who brought together Lusitanians and Celtiberians against Sulla’s regime. In 61–60 BC, **Caesar**, propraetor of the Ulterior province, campaigned against Lusitanian tribes in the Herminian mountains (usually identified with the Serra da Estrela) and went by sea to Brigantium (Corunna). Around this time, or shortly after, the links of **Olisipo** (Lisbon, Felicitas Iulia), **Pax Iulia** (Beja) and Scallabis (Santarém, Praesidium Iulium) with the Julii appear: whether it was Caesar or Augustus who gave them these names is debated.',
  { h: 'The Greek East' },
  'The conquest of the East was made against already weakened Hellenistic kingdoms: Macedon (beaten at Cynoscephalae, 197, and at Pydna, 168), the Syria of Antiochus III (Magnesia, 190), the Pontus of Mithridates VI. After each victory, Rome proclaimed the “freedom” of the Greek cities and, soon after, imposed tutelage. At **Corinth**, in 146, it destroyed a city and its treasures (statues, paintings) were taken to Rome. The Egypt of the Ptolemies, still independent, became dependent on Roman protection.',
  { img: 'rrt-corinto-templo', leg: 'Archaic Temple of Apollo, Ancient Corinth.' },
  { h: 'Gaul' },
  'Until 58 BC Rome controlled only the south of Gaul (Gallia Narbonensis, the “Province”). In eight years, Caesar conquered the rest: the Helvetii, the Belgae, the Veneti, the Aquitani, and invaded Germany and Britain (55–54). The great revolt of **Vercingetorix**, chief of the Arverni, ended at Alesia (52 BC). The figures in the sources (c. 1 million dead and another million enslaved, according to Plutarch) are greatly exaggerated, but the impact was enormous.',
  { img: 'rrt-mapa-galia-cesar', leg: 'Map of Gaul during Caesar’s campaigns, 58–50 BC.' },
  { img: 'rrt-vercingetorix', leg: 'Monument to Vercingetorix, Aimé Millet, 1865; Alise-Sainte-Reine.' },
  { img: 'rrt-alesia-cerco', leg: 'AI-generated illustration. Siege of Alesia, 52 BC; conjectural reconstruction of the fortifications.' },
  { h: 'Rome from within: the Forum and the Curia of Pompey' },
  'The decisive events had precise places. In the **Forum** the tribunes made speeches; Tiberius Gracchus was killed near the Capitol and Gaius on the banks of the Tiber; the heads of the proscribed of Sulla and Antony were displayed on the Rostra (Cicero’s was nailed up there). In 55 BC, Pompey inaugurated Rome’s first stone theatre, in the Campus Martius; the complex included a meeting hall, the **Curia of Pompey**, where, on 15 March 44 BC, Caesar was assassinated. Its ruins lie in today’s Largo di Torre Argentina, among the republican temples of the area.',
  { img: 'rrt-largo-argentina', leg: 'Largo di Torre Argentina, Rome, Republican temples beside the site of Pompey’s Curia.' },
  { h: 'The battlefields of the wars' },
  { tabela: { cab: ['Battle', 'Year', 'Opponents', 'Location today'], linhas: [
    ['Trebia', '218 BC', 'Hannibal against the consul Sempronius Longus', 'Northern Italy'],
    ['Lake Trasimene', '217 BC', 'Hannibal against Flaminius', 'Umbria, Italy'],
    ['Cannae', '216 BC', 'Hannibal against Varro and Aemilius Paullus', 'Apulia, Italy'],
    ['Zama', '202 BC', 'Scipio against Hannibal', 'Tunisia (exact site debated)'],
    ['Pydna', '168 BC', 'Aemilius Paullus against Perseus of Macedon', 'Northern Greece'],
    ['Aquae Sextiae', '102 BC', 'Marius against the Teutones', 'Aix-en-Provence, France'],
    ['Vercellae', '101 BC', 'Marius and Catulus against the Cimbri', 'Northern Italy (site debated)'],
    ['Colline Gate', '82 BC', 'Sulla against the Samnites and the Marians', 'Rome'],
    ['Carrhae', '53 BC', 'The Parthians against Crassus', 'Southeastern Turkey'],
    ['Alesia', '52 BC', 'Caesar against Vercingetorix', 'France'],
    ['Pharsalus', '48 BC', 'Caesar against Pompey', 'Thessaly, Greece'],
    ['Philippi', '42 BC', 'Octavian and Antony against Brutus and Cassius', 'Macedonia, Greece'],
    ['Actium', '31 BC', 'Octavian and Agrippa against Antony and Cleopatra', 'Western coast of Greece']
  ] } },
  { h: 'Routes and frontiers' },
  'The map of the Late Republic is that of a maritime empire. The **sea** was the great highway: grain from Sicily, Egypt and Africa reached Ostia and Pozzuoli; legions crossed the Adriatic via Brundisium. Roman **roads** did the rest: the Appian Way (to the south), the Flaminian Way (to the Adriatic), the Aemilian Way (to the Po valley), the Domitian Way (to Gaul and Spain, c. 118 BC) and the Egnatian Way (through Macedonia, 2nd century BC). There was as yet no fixed frontier or “limes”: Rome had provinces and zones of influence, and its frontier was wherever the legions reached.'
];

const sociedade = [
  'This section describes how people were governed, how they lived and how they fought in the Late Republic: institutions, social classes, religion, economy, slavery, daily life, science, technology and the army. In many respects Rome changed greatly between 264 and 27 BC; wherever possible, the date of the change is given.',
  { h: 'The Senate and the magistrates' },
  'The **Senate** had 300 members, in practice for life (Sulla enlarged it to 600, Caesar to 900). It did not make laws, but advised the magistrates, controlled finance, foreign policy and religion, and allotted the provinces. The **magistrates** were elected for one year: 2 **consuls** (heads of state and army), the **praetors** (justice and governing the provinces), the **aediles** (public works, markets and games), the **quaestors** (finance) and the **censors** (census and morals, elected every five years). The ten **tribunes of the plebs** protected ordinary citizens and could veto the decisions of other magistrates. The career followed a fixed order, the **cursus honorum**, with minimum ages set by law in 180 BC (the lex Villia annalis).',
  { h: 'The assemblies and the vote' },
  'Citizens voted in assemblies: in the **centuriate assembly** (organised by wealth classes, where the rich voted first and often decided) consuls and praetors were elected; in the **tribal assemblies**, tribunes, aediles and quaestors, and laws were voted. To vote you had to be in Rome, which excluded most citizens living in Italy. The ballot was made secret by laws between 139 and 107 BC. Electoral corruption (buying votes, distributing grain and games) was common and led to several laws that were almost never enforced.',
  { h: 'Optimates and populares' },
  'Historians speak of two tendencies: the **optimates** (“the best men”), who defended the power of the Senate, and the **populares**, who appealed to the people against the Senate, usually through the tribunes and the assemblies. They were not organised parties, but methods: Tiberius Gracchus, Marius and Caesar used the popular route, Sulla and Cato the senatorial one. Almost all were rich aristocrats. The struggles were about power, land and provinces, more than about ideology.',
  { img: 'rrt-comicios', leg: 'AI-generated illustration. Voting on the Campus Martius, c. 70 BC; interpretive scene.' },
  { h: 'Society: orders and classes' },
  { lista: [
    '**Senators:** the elite, with fortunes in land; forbidden, by a law of c. 218 BC, from owning large seagoing merchant ships.',
    '**Equestrians (equites):** the second order, of the rich. Many were **publicani**, businessmen who bid, by contract with the state, for tax collection, mines and public works.',
    '**Plebs:** ordinary citizens: peasants, craftsmen, small traders and, more and more, the unemployed in the cities. The urban plebs depended on grain distributions, at least since the laws of Gaius Gracchus (123 BC).',
    '**Italian and Latin allies:** not Roman citizens until 90–88 BC; they paid war taxes and supplied half the army.',
    '**Freedmen:** former slaves who had been freed and became citizens, but without access to magistracies; the sons of freedmen were fully free.',
    '**Slaves:** without rights, the property of the master, who could sell, punish and free them.'
  ] },
  { h: 'Clientage and family' },
  'Roman society ran on **personal relationships**. Every powerful man (the patronus) had clients who greeted him in the morning, supported him in votes and received protection and favours. The basic unit was the **family** (familia), headed by the pater familias, with legal power over children, wife (depending on the type of marriage), slaves and property. Women neither voted nor held office, but those of rich families managed property and exercised political influence; the mother of the Gracchi, **Cornelia**, remained a model, and **Fulvia**, wife of Clodius and then of Antony, was a political figure of weight.',
  { h: 'Land, peasants and latifundia' },
  'This was the central social problem. Long wars took peasants from their land for years and conquered lands, the ager publicus, were occupied by the rich. **Latifundia** worked by slaves arose and impoverished peasants migrated to Rome. This was the problem Tiberius Gracchus tried to answer in 133 BC with a law limiting the public land one individual could occupy (about 500 iugera). Resistance to this law began the series of violent crises.',
  { h: 'Slavery' },
  'Conquest brought an enormous number of slaves into Italy: prisoners of war (150,000 from Epirus in 167 BC, according to the sources, but the figure is disputed), victims of pirates, children of slave women. Modern estimates for Italy in the 1st century BC are much debated, but slavery was a central feature of the economy, and perhaps 20 to 30% of the population. Conditions were harsher in mines and fields than in households. There were three great revolts: two in Sicily (135–132 and 104–100 BC) and that of **Spartacus** (73–71 BC). All were crushed with violence.',
  { img: 'rrt-escravos-minas', leg: 'AI-generated illustration. Enslaved adults working in a silver mine in Hispania, second century BC; interpretive scene.' },
  { h: 'The state religion' },
  'Roman religion was a contractual relationship with the gods, the pax deorum: the state performed the exact rites and the gods gave protection. The **priesthoods** (pontiffs, augurs, Vestals, flamines) were political offices contested by senators: Caesar was elected Pontifex Maximus in 63 BC. Before important decisions the auspices were consulted (flight of birds, appetite of the sacred chickens). The Romans imported gods: **Cybele**, the Great Mother, was brought from Asia Minor in 204 BC, in the midst of the war with Hannibal; in 186 BC the Senate harshly repressed the cult of Bacchus (the Bacchanalia). In moments of extreme crisis, the sources mention human sacrifices of two Greeks and two Gauls, buried alive (in 228, 216 and 114 BC).',
  { tabela: { cab: ['God', 'Greek equivalent', 'Domain', 'Notes'], linhas: [
    ['Jupiter', 'Zeus', 'Sky, thunderbolt, oath', 'Temple on the Capitol, with Juno and Minerva (the Capitoline Triad)'],
    ['Juno', 'Hera', 'Women, marriage', 'Protector of the state'],
    ['Minerva', 'Athena', 'Wisdom, crafts', 'Third of the Triad'],
    ['Mars', 'Ares', 'War', 'Mythical father of Romulus; the Campus Martius bears his name'],
    ['Venus', 'Aphrodite', 'Love, victory', 'Mythical ancestor of the Julii; Caesar dedicated a temple to her in 46 BC'],
    ['Vesta', 'Hestia', 'Hearth of the city', 'Sacred fire kept by the Vestals'],
    ['Ceres', 'Demeter', 'Cereals, harvests', 'Linked to the plebs and the grain supply'],
    ['Cybele (Magna Mater)', 'Cybele', 'Great Mother', 'Cult imported from Phrygia in 204 BC'],
    ['Janus', 'No equivalent', 'Beginnings and passages', 'Two-faced god; gives his name to January']
  ] } },
  { h: 'Economy: tribute, mines and trade' },
  'War was the engine of the economy: spoils, indemnities and provincial tribute enriched the state and the elite. After the victory over Macedon, in 167 BC, Roman citizens stopped paying direct tax, until 43 BC. The **publicani** collected provincial taxes (the tithe in Sicily, the taxes of Asia from 123 BC) and often abused their position. Spain gave silver and other metals (the mines of Cartagena, for example, employed tens of thousands of workers, according to Polybius). Maritime trade grew, with grain from Sicily, Africa and Egypt, wine and oil from Italy, and the ports of Pozzuoli and Ostia.',
  { h: 'Coinage' },
  'The Roman monetary system was modernised during Hannibal’s war: the silver **denarius** (equal to 10 asses, later 16) was introduced around 211 BC and lasted for centuries. From 44 BC, Caesar was the first living Roman to appear on a coin. Coins also served as propaganda: Caesar’s assassins struck a denarius with two daggers and the legend EID MAR.',
  { h: 'Writing, Latin and literature' },
  'Latin was written with an alphabet of 21 letters (later 23), on wax tablets, papyrus and, on monuments, in stone and bronze. Latin literature was born under Greek influence: **Livius Andronicus** translated the Odyssey and produced the first play in Latin (240 BC); **Ennius** wrote the Annals in verse; the comic playwrights **Plautus** and **Terence** adapted Greek plays. In the 1st century BC, Latin prose and poetry reached their peak with **Cicero**, **Caesar**, **Sallust**, **Lucretius**, **Catullus** and **Varro**.',
  { h: 'House and city' },
  'In Rome, most people lived in **insulae**, apartment blocks of several floors, built of brick and timber, dangerous because of the risk of fire and collapse. The rich had **domus**, one-storey houses around an atrium with an impluvium, and villas in the country. The city grew without a plan: narrow, dark streets, great fires, Tiber floods. Aqueducts (the Aqua Marcia, built in 144–140 BC, was the largest to that date) brought water from afar, and the **Cloaca Maxima** drained the Forum.',
  { h: 'Food' },
  'The basis was the trilogy of **cereals, wine and olive oil**. Bread and puls, a wheat porridge, fed the poor; meat was rare. The rich held banquets (convivia) with dishes of game, fish and fruit, and used the fermented fish sauce, garum. Foods from the provinces (figs from Africa, the cherries from Pontus that Lucullus brought to Italy, according to Pliny) made the table more varied. The Senate even passed laws against luxury at table (the leges sumptuariae), with little effect.',
  { h: 'Clothing' },
  'The Roman citizen’s dress was the **toga**, of white wool, which only male citizens could wear in public; underneath, the **tunic**. Magistrates wore the **toga praetexta**, with a purple border; candidates for office, the **toga candida**, whitened (hence “candidate”). Married women wore the **stola** over the tunic, and the palla as a mantle. Triumphant generals wore the purple-and-gold toga. Over the period, silk and Tyrian purple came in as luxuries.',
  { h: 'Education' },
  'Education was private. The sons of the rich learned with pedagogues (often Greek slaves), then with the grammaticus (Greek and Latin literature) and, finally, with the rhetor (rhetoric). It was in the 2nd century BC that Greek culture became the basis of elite education, despite the opposition of Cato the Elder. In 155 BC, an embassy of three Athenian philosophers caused a sensation in Rome, but the Senate sent them away because their dialectic was considered dangerous. Rhetoric was the political art par excellence: Cicero wrote several treatises on it.',
  { h: 'Games, theatre and gladiators' },
  'The **public games** (ludi), paid for by magistrates and generals, were the main entertainment: chariot races in the Circus Maximus, theatre plays and, increasingly, **gladiator** fights. According to tradition, the first combat in Rome was in 264 BC, at a funeral. The origin is debated, perhaps Campania or Etruria. The fights ceased to be exclusively funerary and became political spectacle: Caesar, in 65 BC, gave games with 320 pairs of gladiators. The phrase “Hail, Caesar, those who are about to die salute you” is attested only once, under Claudius, later, and was not a ritual.',
  { img: 'rrt-gladiadores', leg: 'AI-generated illustration. Gladiator training at Capua, c. 75 BC.' },
  { h: 'Science, medicine and technology' },
  'Roman science was above all practical. In medicine, Rome received Greek doctors (tradition says the first was **Archagathus**, in 219 BC), despite Cato’s distrust. The **calendar** was lunar and out of step; in 46 BC, Caesar, advised by the astronomer Sosigenes, created the **Julian calendar**, of 365 days with a leap year every four, which lasted for more than 1,600 years in Europe. Roman engineering used **concrete** (opus caementicium, with volcanic ash from Campania), which allowed vaults and arches. In Rome, **Lucretius** (On the Nature of Things) set out Epicurus’ physics in verse.',
  { h: 'The army: from the manipular legion to Marius’ legion' },
  'Until Marius, the army was a **militia of propertied citizens**, called up in wartime. Polybius’ legion (2nd century BC) had about 4,200 infantry, divided into lines: the young hastati, the experienced principes, the veteran triarii (and the lightly armed velites), organised in **maniples**, plus about 300 cavalry. As wars lengthened, peasants could not be away so long. In 107 BC, Marius recruited landless volunteers (capite censi), according to Sallust: the measure, which tradition presents as a sudden “reform”, was in reality gradual. The legion came to be organised in **cohorts**, with the eagle (aquila) as standard, and soldiers came to depend on the general for pay and retirement land.',
  { img: 'rrt-legionario-mariano', leg: 'AI-generated illustration. Roman legionary of Marius’s era, c. 100 BC; interpretive equipment study.' },
  { h: 'Weapons and tactics' },
  'The soldier carried the scutum (shield), the gladius hispaniensis (short sword, adopted, according to tradition, from Spain), the **pilum** (heavy javelin, which bent on impact) and a mail shirt (lorica hamata). Roman tactics rested on discipline, flexibility of the lines and engineering: fortified camps every night and methodical sieges, as at Syracuse, Numantia and Alesia. Cavalry was supplied largely by the allies.',
  { h: 'The navy' },
  'Rome had no important war fleet until 260 BC, when it built one and adopted the corvus to turn naval combat into infantry combat. It lost entire fleets to storms (more ships than in battle, according to Polybius) and rebuilt them. In the 1st century BC, the fight against the **pirates** and the civil wars (Naulochus, Actium) showed the weight of the sea. The galleys used were triremes and quinqueremes, rowed by free oarsmen, not by chained slaves, contrary to what the cinema shows.',
  { h: 'The state of emergency: dictatorship and senatus consultum ultimum' },
  'The **dictatorship** was an emergency magistracy, lasting six months. It was used throughout the Republic for military crises (Fabius Maximus was dictator in 217). Sulla turned it into an instrument of personal power (82–79) and Caesar had himself appointed dictator several times and, finally, “for life” (44). In parallel, the Senate created in 121 BC the **senatus consultum ultimum**, a decree asking the consuls to “see that the Republic suffered no harm”, which served as justification for killing Gaius Gracchus, the Catilinarians and others. Its critics (Cicero was criticised after 63) said it was illegal to kill citizens without trial.',
  { h: 'Law and the courts' },
  'Roman law was interpreted by magistrates and jurists. At this time the basis was the law of the Twelve Tables (449 BC), to which were added the **edicts of the praetors**, who year after year adapted the rules to new situations. In 149 BC the first permanent court was created (against extortion by governors), followed by others. The **jurists** (Quintus Mucius Scaevola, Servius Sulpicius Rufus) deepened the science of law. Verdicts often depended on the eloquence of the advocates, as Cicero’s speeches against Verres and in defence of Milo and others show.',
  { h: 'The crisis of the Gracchi' },
  'In 133 BC, **Tiberius Gracchus**, tribune of the plebs, presented the land reform, took it directly to the assembly against a colleague’s veto (whom he had removed) and used the Pergamene bequest to fund the distribution. Senators considered him a tyrant and killed him, with hundreds of supporters. In 123–121 BC, his brother **Gaius** launched a much wider programme: subsidised grain, colonies, courts open to the equestrians, roads. He too ended up dead with thousands of followers. These deaths marked the end of the conviction that Roman politics could be settled without shedding citizens’ blood.',
  { h: 'Marius, Sulla and the politics of arms' },
  'It was the generals who changed the system. **Marius** was elected consul seven times (107, 104–100, 86), against tradition. **Sulla** marched on Rome with legionaries, took the city (88 and 82 BC), killed his opponents by proscription and rewrote the Constitution, strengthening the Senate and cutting the powers of the tribunes. He showed that the force of an army could beat the law. After him, **Pompey**, **Crassus** and **Caesar** used the same logic, with the difference that Caesar pardoned his defeated opponents (the clementia Caesaris), even though he ended up murdered by some of them.',
  { img: 'rrt-sepulcro-cipioes', leg: 'Tomb of the Scipios, Rome, Via Appia.' },
  { h: 'Money and politics: the general-entrepreneurs' },
  'Politics was extremely expensive: getting elected consul required millions of sesterces in games, banquets and bribes. Candidates went into debt to gain access to provinces, where they tried to get rich. **Crassus** was the richest man in Rome: he made his fortune from Sulla’s proscriptions, from real estate and from a private fire brigade that bought burning houses for next to nothing. **Caesar** was drowning in debt before Gaul. The defeated in the provinces paid the price.',
  { h: 'Conquests and their price' },
  'There was real profit: tribute, mines, slaves and land. But there was also a social and moral cost. The cities of Italy filled with slaves and the unemployed; soldiers came home without land; the power of governors, far from Rome and without effective supervision, led to abuses like those of Verres. The Roman state did not create a professional administration for the provinces; it entrusted them to annual governors and private contractors. It was this gap, more than any moral vice of the elites, that made the Republic unworkable.',
  { h: 'The calendar and time' },
  'The republican calendar had 355 days and an intercalary month, decided by the pontiffs, who often manipulated it for political reasons, and so in Caesar’s time it was out of step with the seasons by about three months. In 46 BC, the “year of confusion”, with 445 days, restored order. The new calendar came into force on 1 January 45. The month Quintilis was renamed **Julius** (July) in Caesar’s honour, and later Sextilis became Augustus (August).',
  { h: 'Women' },
  'Free Roman women were citizens, but without political rights. They could own property, and some families left them goods by inheritance. Women of the elite had indirect political influence: **Servilia** (mother of Brutus and Caesar’s lover), **Clodia**, the possible “Lesbia” of Catullus (the identification is debated), **Fulvia** and **Cleopatra**, a foreign queen with whom Caesar and Antony had children. In 195 BC, women demonstrated in Rome against the lex Oppia (which limited their luxury), and got it repealed, despite Cato.',
  { h: 'Italy after the Social War' },
  'After the Social War (91–88 BC), most of Italy obtained Roman citizenship. The new citizens were enrolled in only a few tribes, which reduced their weight in votes. In 49 BC citizenship reached the inhabitants of Cisalpine Gaul (north of the Po). This integration, far wider than that of any Greek city, is one of the secrets of Roman success and prepared the later extension of citizenship to the provinces.',
  { h: 'Cities and colonies' },
  'Rome founded colonies of citizens and Latins, at strategic points in Italy and, later, in the provinces: Narbo Martius, in Gaul (118 BC), Italica (206 BC, a settlement for veterans) in Spain, Corinth (44 BC, by Caesar) and Carthage (Caesar’s plans and Augustus’ realisation). Colonies served to solve the land problem (settling veterans and the poor) and to watch over the territory. It was by this route that Roman language, law and culture spread.',
  { h: 'Propaganda and the image of power' },
  'Generals used art and architecture to promote themselves. **Triumphs** and public buildings (temples, theatres, basilicas, porticoes) brought fame. Pompey built the first stone theatre in Rome (55 BC); Caesar, the Forum of Caesar (46 BC), with a temple of Venus Genetrix. **Coins** came to carry portraits of the living. Stone portraits, in a realist tradition (wrinkled heads of elderly aristocrats), give way to more idealised portraits in the Hellenistic fashion.',
  { h: 'Private life, debt and crisis' },
  'Indebtedness was a chronic problem. In 63 BC, Catiline promised to cancel debts (tabulae novae), with the support of the bankrupt and of desperate peasants; he was defeated, but the problem persisted. The elites were in debt too: Caesar owed immense sums before leaving for Gaul. The Roman state, however, had no public bank; loans were private, with a usual legal limit of 12% a year, which in practice was often exceeded.',
  { h: 'Greek culture and resistance' },
  'Rome absorbed Greek culture: art, philosophy, medicine, rhetoric, theatre. Statues and paintings taken from Syracuse, Corinth and Pergamum decorated temples and houses. Some, like Cato the Elder, resisted and considered this luxury a threat to ancestral customs (mos maiorum). Others, like the Scipios, became patrons of poets and philosophers. By the end of the Republic, being Roman and being cultured meant mastering Greek: Cicero studied in Athens and Rhodes and wrote philosophical treatises adapting Greek ideas into Latin.',
  { h: 'Cleopatra, Egypt and grain' },
  'The Egypt of the Ptolemies, the richest Hellenistic kingdom, was the great supplier of grain. The last kings depended on Rome’s protection; Ptolemy XII was restored to his throne in 55 BC with Roman troops. **Cleopatra VII** (69–30 BC) ruled with her brother and husband Ptolemy XIII; she was expelled, and Caesar, who supported her in the Alexandrian war (48–47), installed her on the throne. She had a son by him, **Caesarion** (47 BC). She then allied with Antony, by whom she had three children. Her fall in 30 BC put an end to the last Hellenistic kingdom.',
  { h: 'The Principate in the making' },
  'Octavian, Caesar’s great-nephew and adopted son, was 18 when Caesar died. In 13 years he eliminated Antony and all rivals. In 27 BC, instead of presenting himself as king or dictator (hated names), he presented himself as **princeps** (“first citizen”), keeping consuls and Senate, but retaining command of the legions and the provinces. The formula solved the problem the Republic had failed to solve: how to govern an empire while maintaining the fiction of republican government. See **The High Empire**.'
];

const personalidades = [
  'These 15 figures shaped the history of the Late Republic. Some were heroes to the Romans, others villains; almost all were both, depending on who was telling the story. Biographical details come from ancient sources, not always reliable, and birth dates are often uncertain.',
  { h: 'Publius Cornelius Scipio, “Africanus” (c. 236 – 183 BC)' },
  'The general who defeated Hannibal. In his twenties, he took Cartagena (209 BC), expelled the Carthaginians from Spain (206) and took the war to Africa, where he won at Zama (202). He received the name “Africanus”. He was consul twice, but ended up accused of corruption by his political enemies and retired to Liternum, in Campania, where he died. He was admired by the Greeks and a patron of Hellenistic culture. His adoptive grandson, Scipio Aemilianus, would destroy Carthage and Numantia.',
  { h: 'Cato the Elder (234 – 149 BC)' },
  'A new man from Tusculum, he was consul (195), censor (184) and the model of the austere, conservative Roman. He opposed luxury and Greek influence, which did not stop him from learning Greek. He wrote the oldest surviving Latin prose treatise, De Agri Cultura. Tradition attributes to him the refrain “Carthage must be destroyed” at the end of his speeches. He also supported the lex Voconia, which limited women’s inheritance rights. For posterity he is the symbol of ancient republican virtue, but he was not always portrayed that way by his contemporaries.',
  { h: 'Hannibal Barca (247 – c. 183 BC)' },
  'The Carthaginian general who nearly destroyed Rome. Son of Hamilcar Barca, he swore at nine, according to tradition, to be an eternal enemy of Rome. He commanded the Carthaginians in Spain, invaded Italy by crossing the Alps (218 BC) and beat the Romans at the Trebia, Trasimene and Cannae. For fifteen years he stayed in Italy without managing to take Rome. Defeated by Scipio at Zama, he served Carthage as a magistrate and then fled, in exile, to the East. Pursued by Rome, he killed himself with poison in Bithynia, c. 183 BC. The Romans, who feared him, were among those who admired him most.',
  { h: 'The Gracchi: Tiberius (c. 163 – 133) and Gaius (c. 154 – 121 BC)' },
  'Brothers of the Sempronii Gracchi family, grandsons of Scipio Africanus on their mother’s side, sons of **Cornelia**. Tiberius, tribune in 133 BC, proposed land reform and was killed by senators; Gaius, tribune in 123 and 122, proposed cheap grain, colonies, equestrian courts, and died in 121 BC. Ancient historians (Plutarch, Appian) saw them as heroes or demagogues. For moderns, they were reformers who exposed the problems of a changing Italy, and their deaths marked the start of the period of political violence.',
  { img: 'rrt-gracos', leg: 'The Gracchi, sculptural group by Eugène Guillaume, 1853, Musée d’Orsay, Paris.' },
  { h: 'Gaius Marius (c. 157 – 86 BC)' },
  'From Arpinum, a “new man” with no senatorial ancestors, he married a Julia, Caesar’s aunt. He was consul seven times. He ended the Jugurthine War (105), beat the Cimbri and Teutones (102–101) and reorganised the army. In 88 BC, the dispute with Sulla over command of the war against Mithridates led to civil war. Marius and his ally Cinna took Rome in 87 and massacred opponents, and Marius died on 13 January 86, already in his seventh consulship. He was a great general and a ruthless politician.',
  { img: 'rrt-mario-busto', leg: 'Bust traditionally called “Marius”, Glyptothek, Munich (319). The identification is uncertain.' },
  { h: 'Lucius Cornelius Sulla (138 – 78 BC)' },
  'An impoverished aristocrat, he became Marius’ quaestor and was the one who received Jugurtha’s surrender (105). He won in the Social War and, consul in 88, was the first to march on Rome. He fought Mithridates in Greece and Asia, returned in 83 and won the civil war. As dictator (82–79), he proscribed thousands, rewrote the constitution and abdicated. The Romans called him Felix (“fortunate”). His march showed that the army could decide politics, and he was the model, and the warning, for those who came after.',
  { img: 'rrt-sula-busto', leg: 'Bust traditionally called “Sulla”, Glyptothek, Munich (309). The identification is uncertain.' },
  { h: 'Gnaeus Pompeius Magnus, Pompey the Great (106 – 48 BC)' },
  'He began his career very young, with a private army, at Sulla’s side, and Sulla called him Magnus (“the Great”). He ended the war against Sertorius in Spain (72), finished the revolt of Spartacus (71), cleared the Mediterranean of pirates in three months (67), defeated Mithridates and reorganised the East (66–62). He was the most powerful man in Rome, but could not turn prestige into lasting politics. He married Julia, Caesar’s daughter. Defeated at Pharsalus (48), he was killed at Pelusium on arriving in Egypt, on the orders of King Ptolemy XIII.',
  { img: 'rrt-pompeu-busto', leg: 'Portrait attributed to Pompey; Ny Carlsberg Glyptotek, Copenhagen.' },
  { h: 'Marcus Licinius Crassus (c. 115 – 53 BC)' },
  'The richest man in Rome. He supported Sulla, grew rich from the proscriptions and from real estate, and beat Spartacus (71), with brutal methods, including the decimation of his own troops, according to Plutarch and Appian. He was the third member of the pact with Pompey and Caesar. In 53 BC, at the head of an army in Syria, he wanted the military glory of a war against the Parthians and was defeated at Carrhae, where he died with his son Publius. Tradition, probably apocryphal, says the Parthians poured molten gold into his mouth.',
  { img: 'rrt-crasso-busto', leg: 'Portrait of an unknown Roman, 1st century BC, Louvre (MR 510), formerly attributed to Crassus, Galba or Piso.' },
  { h: 'Marcus Tullius Cicero (106 – 43 BC)' },
  'The greatest Roman orator and the most important Latin prose writer. A native of Arpinum, a “new man”, he reached the consulship in 63 BC and denounced Catiline’s conspiracy, which made him “father of the fatherland” for some and a tyrant for others (he executed conspirators without trial, and was exiled in 58). He prosecuted Verres (70). He was a defender of the Republic, but a hesitant one: he followed Pompey, accepted Caesar’s pardon, attacked Antony in the Philippics. He was killed on Antony’s orders, on 7 December 43 BC, and his head was displayed on the Rostra. About 900 letters have come down to us, an unrivalled source.',
  { img: 'rrt-cicero-busto', leg: 'Bust of Cicero; Capitoline Museums, Rome.' },
  { h: 'Gaius Julius Caesar (100 – 44 BC)' },
  'A patrician of an ancient but not very powerful family, Marius’ nephew by marriage, he narrowly escaped Sulla’s proscriptions. He was pontifex maximus (63), consul (59), governor of Gaul (58–50), conqueror of Gaul and winner of the civil war (49–45). He wrote the Commentaries with great political skill. As dictator, he reformed the calendar, enlarged the Senate, founded colonies and planned campaigns in Dacia and against the Parthians. He was assassinated on 15 March 44 BC by senators who accused him of wanting to be king, although he refused the crown at the Lupercalia festival, according to the sources. His birth date, 100 or 102 BC, is disputed.',
  { img: 'rrt-cesar-busto', leg: 'Bust of Julius Caesar; Vatican Museums.' },
  { h: 'Spartacus (d. 71 BC)' },
  'A Thracian by birth, he was a soldier, prisoner and then gladiator in a school at Capua. In 73 BC, with about seventy companions, he escaped and formed an army of slaves. He beat several legions in southern Italy. We do not know what he wanted: to leave Italy (most likely, according to Plutarch), or to plunder. He was defeated by Crassus and died in battle, his body never identified. His figure, of enormous cultural impact, has little basis in the sources: the accounts of Plutarch and Appian are scant and were written by Romans.',
  { img: 'rrt-espartaco-estatua', leg: 'Spartacus breaking his chains, Denis Foyatier, 1830; Louvre.' },
  { h: 'Viriathus (d. 139 BC)' },
  'A Lusitanian chief, of humble origin; ancient sources do not say where he was born, and modern tradition points to the Serra da Estrela region. He escaped Galba’s massacre and, c. 147 BC, was elected chief of the Lusitanians. For eight years, with small, fast bands, he beat the Roman praetors in Spain. The Romans had to offer him a treaty, which they later did not honour. He was killed by treachery in 139 BC. His memory was revived in the 16th century and, above all, in the 19th and 20th, as a Portuguese national symbol, although the Lusitanians were not “Portuguese” in the modern sense.',
  { h: 'Cleopatra VII Philopator (69 – 30 BC)' },
  'The last queen of Ptolemaic Egypt. A descendant of Ptolemy I, she was the only one of her dynasty to learn Egyptian, according to Plutarch. With Caesar’s support, she beat her brother in the civil war (47 BC); she then joined Mark Antony and used Roman power to try to restore the Ptolemaic empire. Defeated at Actium, she killed herself in 30 BC. Tradition, from Plutarch, speaks of an asp; moderns debate whether it might have been poison. Octavian’s propaganda painted her as a dangerous, seductive foreigner, an image literature and cinema perpetuated.',
  { img: 'rrt-cleopatra-busto', leg: 'Diademed bust of Cleopatra VII; Altes Museum, Berlin.' },
  { h: 'Mark Antony (83 – 30 BC)' },
  'Soldier and orator, Caesar’s right-hand man in Gaul and in the civil war, consul in 44 BC. He delivered Caesar’s funeral oration and stirred the people against the conspirators. Ally of Octavian and Lepidus in the Second Triumvirate, he won at Philippi (42). He took the East and joined Cleopatra. The campaign against the Parthians (36) failed, and Octavian’s propaganda accused him of having “gone Greek” and of wanting to divide the empire. Defeated at Actium, he killed himself in Alexandria. His image, like Cleopatra’s, was written by the victors.',
  { img: 'rrt-antonio-busto', leg: 'Male bust traditionally attributed to Mark Antony, Vatican Museums. The Commons record dates it to the Flavian period (AD 69–96); the identification is uncertain.' },
  { h: 'Gaius Julius Caesar Octavianus, later Augustus (63 BC – AD 14)' },
  'Caesar’s great-nephew, adopted by him in his will. He was 18 when his uncle died. In the next seventeen years, he allied with Antony and Lepidus, avenged his uncle, eliminated his opponents and, at Actium (31), with Agrippa, beat Antony. In 27 BC, with the formula of the princeps and the “restoration of the Republic”, he created the regime that lasted about five centuries in the West. He was ruthless in the proscriptions of 43 and moderate afterwards. This page ends here; his long life as ruler is told in **The High Empire**.',
  { img: 'rrt-octaviano-busto', leg: 'Portrait of the young Octavian, around 31 BC, Capitoline Museums, photographed at an exhibition in the Grand Palais, Paris.' }
];

const legado = [
  { h: 'What they left us' },
  'The Late Republic left us classical Latin, Roman law in formation, the model of the empire and a political experience of centuries, studied by all who later thought about the limits of power. It also left the works of **Cicero**, **Caesar**, **Lucretius** and **Sallust**, still read today, and a tradition of monuments and coins. It left a warning too: how institutions corrode when political agents stop respecting the rules.',
  { h: 'The fall of the Republic: explanations' },
  'Why did it fall? There is no single answer. Historians point to: the **size of the empire** and the lack of a professional administration; **armies loyal to generals** and not to the state; **inequality** and the land problem; **competition** between aristocrats for prestige and wealth; **political violence** without punishment. For Sallust, it was the moral corruption that followed the destruction of Carthage (the thesis of metus hostilis); for moderns, a combination of structural causes and individual choices. There was contingency: had Caesar not been assassinated, or had Antony won at Actium, history would have been different.',
  { img: 'rrt-morte-cesar', leg: 'The Death of Caesar, Jean-Léon Gérôme, 1867; Walters Art Museum.' },
  { h: 'Caesar’s assassination and the myth of Brutus' },
  'The conspirators, who called themselves “liberators”, thought they were restoring the Republic and did not realise the regime was already dead. The phrase “Et tu, Brute?” is Shakespeare’s, not from the ancient sources; Suetonius reports that Caesar may have said, in Greek, “you too, my child?”, but presents it as a version in circulation. Tyrannicide (killing a tyrant) has been a political theme ever since: from Dante (who put Brutus and Cassius in the last circle of Hell) to the republicans of the 18th century.',
  { h: 'Cicero and literature' },
  'Cicero’s texts (speeches, letters, philosophical treatises) were the model of Latin prose and of European rhetoric. Petrarch, in the 14th century, rediscovered his letters, which was a landmark of Humanism. **Lucretius’** poem on Epicureanism, nearly lost, was found in 1417 by the humanist Poggio Bracciolini. Caesar’s Commentaries are still today a model of clear prose.',
  { img: 'rrt-cicero-catilina', leg: 'Cicero Denounces Catiline, Cesare Maccari, 1888; Palazzo Madama, Rome.' },
  { h: 'Law and institutions' },
  'The idea of **annual magistracies**, of **collegiality** (two consuls watching each other) and of **veto**, the practice of the **assembly**, the concept of **republic**: all this was read, in the Renaissance and afterwards, as an alternative to monarchy. Polybius, who described the Roman Constitution as mixed (monarchy, aristocracy, democracy), influenced Montesquieu and the founders of the United States, who divided power into branches and called the upper house the “Senate”. The terms “consul”, “dictator” and “tribune” were used in the French and American revolutions, and later in the 20th century.',
  { h: 'Expansion and the Mediterranean' },
  'The conquest of Greece and the East put Rome in contact with Hellenism, and the Mediterranean became, as the Romans said, mare nostrum. The political unity that resulted (roads, language, coinage, law) allowed the circulation of people, ideas and religions, including, later, Christianity. The Mediterranean would not be a political unit again until the Roman Empire.',
  { img: 'rrt-actium-batalha', leg: 'AI-generated illustration. Battle of Actium, 31 BC; artistic interpretation.' },
  { img: 'rrt-rubicao', leg: 'AI-generated illustration. Caesar at the Rubicon, January 49 BC, before crossing; interpretive scene.' },
  { h: 'Myths of the Republic' },
  { lista: [
    '**“Carthage must be destroyed” and the salt:** the first is attributed to Cato by the sources (Plutarch, Pliny); the story of salt thrown on the fields to make them barren appears only in the 19th century.',
    '**Gladiators and the thumb:** there is no proof that thumbs down meant death. The gesture is debated and the image comes from 19th-century painting.',
    '**“Alea iacta est” (the die is cast):** it is tradition; Suetonius gives the phrase in Latin, and Plutarch quotes it in Greek. Nobody knows the exact form.',
    '**“Veni, vidi, vici” (I came, I saw, I conquered):** Plutarch says Caesar wrote it in a letter to a friend about the victory at Zela (47 BC); Suetonius says it was displayed in his triumph.',
    '**Spartacus, hero of freedom:** a modern construction, mostly of the 18th and 19th centuries, with little basis in the ancient sources.',
    '**Cleopatra and the nose:** Pascal’s famous remark (Cleopatra’s nose) is a reflection on history, and proves nothing about her appearance. Portraits on coins show a strong profile, with a long nose and a firm chin.'
  ] },
  { h: 'What changed in Portugal' },
  'From 206 BC (Italica) Spain was Romanised. The conquest of Lusitania began with the wars of Viriathus and ended, with the pacification of the northwest, in Augustus’ time. The Latin language, which gave rise to Portuguese, Roman law, the road network, the cities (Olisipo, Pax Iulia, Scallabis, Aeminium, Bracara Augusta, the last ones already in the Empire) and Christianity have roots in this process. But it must be remembered that pre-Roman peoples had their own languages and cultures, and that “Romanisation” was gradual and uneven.',
  { h: 'Where to see the Late Republic' },
  { lista: [
    '**Rome:** the Roman Forum and the Capitol; Largo di Torre Argentina (the Curia of Pompey, where Caesar was killed); the tomb of the Scipios, on the Appian Way; the Appian Way; the Capitoline Museums and the Vatican Museums (portraits of Pompey, Caesar, Cicero).',
    '**Carthage (Tunisia):** archaeological site and museum, with the hills of Byrsa and the tophet (Punic ruins beneath the Roman ones).',
    '**Spain:** Numantia (Soria), Italica (Santiponce, Seville), the Cartagena Museum (Carthago Nova).',
    '**France:** the Alésia MuséoParc (Alise-Sainte-Reine), near the traditional site of the siege of 52 BC.',
    '**Portugal:** in Lisbon, the Museu de Lisboa — Roman Theatre and the National Archaeological Museum; in Viseu, the statue of Viriathus and the Grão Vasco Museum. There are no secure material remains of Viriathus.',
    '**Greece:** ancient Corinth, Pharsalus (battlefield), Philippi (archaeological site) and Nicopolis (near Preveza), the city Octavian founded to commemorate Actium.'
  ] },
  { h: 'Open questions' },
  { lista: [
    '**Marius’ “reforms”:** was it sudden (107 BC), or gradual? Modern scholars tend to see it as a process.',
    '**Caesar’s birth date** (100 or 102 BC), and Cleopatra’s: estimates.',
    '**The numbers** of dead at Cannae, Alesia and of Spartacus’ army: always exaggerated in ancient sources.',
    '**The site of the battle of Zama**, of Vercellae, and of many others: disputed.',
    '**Who founded** Olisipo as Felicitas Iulia and Pax Iulia: Caesar or Augustus?',
    '**Cleopatra’s death:** asp, poison, or murder ordered by Octavian? The official version was suicide, but some suspect otherwise.'
  ] },
  { h: 'Recommended reading' },
  { lista: [
    'Polybius, Histories; Livy, From the Founding of the City; Appian, Civil Wars; Plutarch, Parallel Lives; Caesar, Commentaries; Cicero, Letters; Sallust, The Conspiracy of Catiline and The Jugurthine War.',
    'Mary Beard, SPQR: A History of Ancient Rome (2015); Tom Holland, Rubicon (2003); Adrian Goldsworthy, The Fall of Carthage (2000) and Caesar: Life of a Colossus (2006); Ronald Syme, The Roman Revolution (1939); Harriet Flower, Roman Republics (2010).'
  ] }
];

